{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for draft4 of notes/2026-10-19-record-and-event-identifiers.md at88056d593775371a76cb902df7da9ffadc1cddc3/treeeecf190da68122163354ba610292ee75054422fc, primaryb7a1fecf. Read all519lines, part one cold first, then adoption and sources. Four required changes; complete finding set.\n\nF1 [P1] A held event is not yet a membership anchor (section4, line201 and lines220-222).\nThe level2 stopping rule permits reaching an event the verifier already holds, but the table then claims a chain from the cited genesis. Holding bytes or previously passing levels0/1 establishes neither that ancestor's membership in this record nor its cited position. For example, a hash-correct entry can name rec=A while linking at seq1 to an already-held genesis from B; stopping merely because B is cached cannot prove membership in A. Specify that an early anchor is an exact identity/position/digest tuple already proved to that same genesis, or explicitly trusted by the caller AS a membership claim for that record. Validate the anchor tuple and expose the unproved premise in trusts; do not report an unconditional genesis-chain conclusion for arbitrary cached bytes. Define which checks apply to every traversed predecessor, not just the target. Include wrong-record cached anchors and a cached bytes-only/claim-only entry in conformance. An unavailable predecessor or shallow boundary must not become such an anchor merely because the walk ended.\n\nF2 [P1] The worked Git example does not perform its claimed byte verification (section4, lines228-233).\ngit cat-file -t reports an object's type; it does not establish that the content hashes to the requested object name. I reproduced this in a new owned scratch bare repository: replaced one loose commit object's compressed content at its existing name with another valid commit of equal length; cat-file -t returned exit0 and commit, cat-file commit returned the altered bytes with exit0, while rehashing those bytes yielded a different object ID and git fsck reported hash-path mismatch/exit1. Requested32ed63af5facb0e5437924295459fc39246b1d04; served bytes hash tob5348131fdc553110a5046806dc478018146d1c7. The probe touched no project Git data.\nReplace the example with an actual raw-content hash check for the target AND each predecessor used in level2. Its walks must also disable replace refs/grafts as the format requires; the current rev-list commands do not show that protection. Keep missing/shallow history distinct from successful membership. Primary references: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-cat-file (-t is type output), https://git-scm.com/docs/git-fsck (hash mismatch integrity errors). A valid signature is likewise not a substitute for checking the cited object's hash.\n\nF3 [P2] Supply a complete conformance/profile contract before claiming independently implementable published formats (sections3.1-3.4 and7).\nThe three stated vector directories contain no files in the exact candidate: git ls-tree under vectors returns nothing. dap1 vectors are deferred to the future section8 implementation, and the published protocol part points into that adoption section. Section7's per-case input has only one event or null plus citation/expect; it cannot unambiguously select a different predecessor-chain view, trust anchors or signature-rule inputs for the required replaced-middle case and levels2/3. Provide fixed independently produced bytes/digests/results and enough per-case source/trust inputs to reproduce each verification result; include malformed syntax, missing history, wrong anchors and signatures, not only good byte hashes.\nAlso make the claimed format definitions self-contained or cite a versioned normative profile: dap1 must identify the exact signed JSON member, public-key/signature encodings and extraction/verification rule; git's choice of gpgsig versus application-specific trailers needs an identifiable genesis convention/profile and exact covered bytes for claimed level3 support. Saying that a genesis names a rule without defining how to name/read it leaves an outsider guessing. Retain the honest unsupported-rule stop at2.\nThe registry says a token without all nine parts is not a format and is allocated only with definitions/vectors, but calls at/nostr allocated/registered while their definitions are owed. Mark those rows reserved/proposed, rather than claiming a completed format. Keep the decided seq method and scope-in-reference choices; this finding does not reopen them.\n\nF4 [P2] Finish the wire and result shapes so independent implementations agree (sections4,5.2,5.3).\nThe report lacks typed values for reached/anchor/trusts and the case where level0 fails before ANY level passed; it also does not distinguish an unavailable predecessor/unsupported signature rule/requested verification depth from a proved mismatch. Specify deterministic success, mismatch and incomplete/unsupported examples, with the level actually proved. The vector expect object needs the same exact contract.\nDefine base64 alphabet/padding and exact utf-8 reconstruction, the full record.genesis member spelling, head type, pagination count/cursor/end behavior and invalid-input responses. A concise complete JSON example/schema is enough. For a DID document, every service map also needs its own unique URI id; the described type+serviceEndpoint alone omits a required field. Use the appropriate DID JSON representation/media type or explicitly identify an HTTP wrapper, and explain how the DID URL path dereferences through the advertised base binding. DID Core's service requirements and representation rules support this: https://www.w3.org/TR/did-core/#services and https://www.w3.org/TR/did-core/#representations. The event-as-path decision itself is compatible with DID URL syntax.\n\nArchitecture: separating immutable genesis identity, host address, event digest and system-specific judgment is a clear foundation. Keeping the scope ID for routing avoids inventing a